Building OpenOCD for Windows
----------------------------

You can build OpenOCD for Windows natively with either MinGW-w64/MSYS
or Cygwin (plain MinGW might work with --disable-werror but is not
recommended as it doesn't provide enough C99 compatibility).
Alternatively, one can cross-compile it using MinGW-w64 on a *nix
host. See README for the generic instructions.

Also, the MSYS2 project provides both ready-made binaries and an easy
way to self-compile from their software repository out of the box.

Native MinGW-w64/MSYS compilation
-----------------------------

As MSYS doesn't come with pkg-config pre-installed, you need to add it
manually. The easiest way to do that is to download pkg-config-lite
from:

  http://sourceforge.net/projects/pkgconfiglite/

Then simply unzip the archive to the root directory of your MinGW-w64
installation.

USB adapters
------------

For the adapters that use a HID-based protocol, e.g. CMSIS-DAP, you do
not need to perform any additional configuration.

For all the others you usually need to have WinUSB.sys (or
libusbK.sys) driver installed. Some vendor software (e.g. for
ST-LINKv2) does it on its own. For the other cases the easiest way to
assign WinUSB to a device is to use the latest Zadig installer:

  http://zadig.akeo.ie

When using a composite USB device, it's often necessary to assign
WinUSB.sys to the composite parent instead of the specific
interface. To do that one needs to activate an advanced option in the
Zadig installer.

If you need to use the same adapter with other applications that may
require another driver, a solution for Windows Vista and above is to
activate the IgnoreHWSerNum registry setting for the USB device.

That setting forces Windows to associate the driver per port instead of
per serial number, the same behaviour as when the device does not contain
a serial number. So different drivers can be installed for the adapter on
different ports and you just need to plug the adapter into the correct
port depending on which application to use.

For more information, see:

  https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/usbcon/usb-device-specific-registry-settings
  http://www.ftdichip.com/Support/Knowledgebase/index.html?ignorehardwareserialnumber.htm

Building OpenOCD for Windows by cross-compiling from Linux
----------------------------------------------------------

You can cross-compile Mindgrove OpenOCD for 64-bit Windows (`x86_64-w64-mingw32`) on an Ubuntu/Debian host. This process builds static dependencies (`libusb` and `libftdi`), links them into OpenOCD, and bundles `libftdi1.dll`.

### 1. Install Prerequisites

Install the MinGW-w64 cross-compiler, build tools, and testing utilities:

```bash
sudo apt-get update
sudo DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive apt-get install -y \
    autoconf automake autotools-dev libtool pkg-config cmake \
    build-essential bison flex texinfo gperf patchutils bc gawk \
    git wget curl unzip p7zip-full zip python3 \
    mingw-w64 binutils-mingw-w64 gcc-mingw-w64 g++-mingw-w64 \
    libz-mingw-w64-dev nsis wine xvfb

```

### 2. Set Up Environment Variables & Directories

Define your target host, job count, and workspace paths:

```bash
export WORKSPACE="$(pwd)"
export RISCV_ROOT="${WORKSPACE}/riscv_windows_build"
export TARGET_HOST="x86_64-w64-mingw32"
export JOBS="$(nproc)"

mkdir -p "${RISCV_ROOT}/src" "${RISCV_ROOT}/build" "${RISCV_ROOT}/install" "${RISCV_ROOT}/deps"

```

### 3. Clone Source Repositories

Fetch the Mindgrove OpenOCD repository and its required USB drivers:

```bash
cd "${RISCV_ROOT}/src"

# Clone OpenOCD (Latest tag)
git clone --depth 1 --branch <Latest Tag> https://github.com/Mindgrove-Technologies/riscv-openocd.git riscv-openocd

# Clone USB libraries
git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/mcuee/libftdi.git libftdi
git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/libusb/libusb.git libusb

```

### 4. Build `libusb` (Static)

Compile a static `libusb` library for Windows:

```bash
cd "${RISCV_ROOT}/src/libusb"

if [ ! -f configure ]; then
    autoreconf -fi
fi

rm -rf "${RISCV_ROOT}/build/libusb-mingw"
mkdir -p "${RISCV_ROOT}/build/libusb-mingw"
cd "${RISCV_ROOT}/build/libusb-mingw"

../../src/libusb/configure \
    --host="${TARGET_HOST}" \
    --prefix="${RISCV_ROOT}/deps/libusb" \
    --enable-static --disable-shared

make -j"${JOBS}"
make install

# Create pkg-config definition
cat > "${RISCV_ROOT}/deps/libusb/lib/pkgconfig/libusb-1.0.pc" <<EOF
prefix=${RISCV_ROOT}/deps/libusb
exec_prefix=\${prefix}
libdir=\\${exec_prefix}/lib
includedir=\\${prefix}/include/libusb-1.0

Name: libusb-1.0
Description: C API for USB device access (MinGW cross)
Version: 1.0.27
Libs: -L\\${libdir} -lusb-1.0
Cflags: -I\\${includedir}
EOF

```

### 5. Build `libftdi` & Prepare DLL

Compile `libftdi` using CMake with MinGW cross-compilation settings and copy `libftdi1.dll` to the distribution folder:

```bash
cd "${RISCV_ROOT}"

# Create CMake toolchain file for MinGW
cat > "${RISCV_ROOT}/src/mingw-toolchain.cmake" <<EOF
set(CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME Windows)
set(CMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR x86_64)
set(CMAKE_C_COMPILER   ${TARGET_HOST}-gcc)
set(CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER ${TARGET_HOST}-g++)
set(CMAKE_RC_COMPILER  ${TARGET_HOST}-windres)
set(CMAKE_FIND_ROOT_PATH /usr/${TARGET_HOST})
set(CMAKE_FIND_ROOT_PATH_MODE_PROGRAM NEVER)
set(CMAKE_FIND_ROOT_PATH_MODE_LIBRARY BOTH)
set(CMAKE_FIND_ROOT_PATH_MODE_INCLUDE BOTH)
set(CMAKE_FIND_ROOT_PATH_MODE_PACKAGE BOTH)
set(CMAKE_EXE_LINKER_FLAGS "-static-libgcc -static-libstdc++ -static")
EOF

rm -rf "${RISCV_ROOT}/build/libftdi-mingw"
mkdir -p "${RISCV_ROOT}/build/libftdi-mingw"
cd "${RISCV_ROOT}/build/libftdi-mingw"

cmake "${RISCV_ROOT}/src/libftdi" \
    -DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE="${RISCV_ROOT}/src/mingw-toolchain.cmake" \
    -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX="${RISCV_ROOT}/deps/libftdi" \
    -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
    -DDOCUMENTATION=OFF -DFTDI_EEPROM=OFF -DEXAMPLES=OFF -DFTDIPP=OFF \
    -DLIBUSB_INCLUDE_DIR="${RISCV_ROOT}/deps/libusb/include/libusb-1.0" \
    -DLIBUSB_LIBRARIES="${RISCV_ROOT}/deps/libusb/lib/libusb-1.0.a"

make -j"${JOBS}"
make install

ln -sf "${RISCV_ROOT}/deps/libftdi/lib/libftdi1.a" "${RISCV_ROOT}/deps/libftdi/lib/libftdi.a"

# Pre-copy libftdi1.dll for final distribution
mkdir -p "${RISCV_ROOT}/install/openocd/bin"
cp "${RISCV_ROOT}/deps/libftdi/bin/libftdi1.dll" "${RISCV_ROOT}/install/openocd/bin/"

# Create pkg-config definition
cat > "${RISCV_ROOT}/deps/libftdi/lib/pkgconfig/libftdi1.pc" <<EOF
prefix=${RISCV_ROOT}/deps/libftdi
exec_prefix=\${prefix}
libdir=\\${exec_prefix}/lib
includedir=\\${prefix}/include/libftdi1

Name: libftdi1
Description: Library to program and debug FTDI USB chips
Version: 1.5
Libs: -L\\${libdir} -lftdi1 -L${RISCV_ROOT}/deps/libusb/lib -lusb-1.0
Cflags: -I\\${includedir}
EOF

```

### 6. Build OpenOCD

Configure and compile Mindgrove OpenOCD targeting Windows:

```bash
export PKG_CONFIG_PATH="${RISCV_ROOT}/deps/libusb/lib/pkgconfig:${RISCV_ROOT}/deps/libftdi/lib/pkgconfig"
export PKG_CONFIG_LIBDIR=""
export PKG_CONFIG_SYSROOT_DIR="/"

MOCK_INC="${RISCV_ROOT}/deps/mock_include"
mkdir -p "$MOCK_INC"
cp /usr/include/elf.h "$MOCK_INC/elf.h"

cd "${RISCV_ROOT}/src/riscv-openocd"
git reset --hard
git clean -fdx

git submodule sync --recursive
git submodule update --init --recursive --remote --force
./bootstrap

rm -rf "${RISCV_ROOT}/build/riscv-openocd"
mkdir -p "${RISCV_ROOT}/build/riscv-openocd"
cd "${RISCV_ROOT}/build/riscv-openocd"

../../src/riscv-openocd/configure \
    --host="${TARGET_HOST}" \
    --prefix="${RISCV_ROOT}/install/openocd" \
    --enable-internal-jimtcl \
    --with-jimtcl-opts="--disable-ssl --disable-ipv6 --host=${TARGET_HOST}" \
    --enable-internal-libjaylink \
    --enable-ftdi \
    --enable-jlink \
    --disable-werror \
    CPPFLAGS="-D_WIN32_WINNT=0x0600 -I$MOCK_INC -I${RISCV_ROOT}/deps/libusb/include/libusb-1.0 -I${RISCV_ROOT}/deps/libftdi/include/libftdi1" \
    LDFLAGS="-L${RISCV_ROOT}/deps/libusb/lib -L${RISCV_ROOT}/deps/libftdi/lib -static-libgcc -static-libstdc++" \
    LIBS="-lsetupapi -lole32 -lwinusb -lws2_32" \
    CFLAGS="-g -O2"

make -j"${JOBS}"
make install

# Ensure libftdi1.dll exists alongside openocd.exe in bin/
mkdir -p "${RISCV_ROOT}/install/openocd/bin"
cp "${RISCV_ROOT}/deps/libftdi/bin/libftdi1.dll" "${RISCV_ROOT}/install/openocd/bin/"

```

### 7. Verification via Wine (Optional)

You can verify that the Windows binary executes properly under Linux using Wine:

```bash
INSTALL="${RISCV_ROOT}/install"
export WINEPREFIX="${RISCV_ROOT}/build/wine_prefix"
export WINEDEBUG=-all
rm -rf "$WINEPREFIX"
mkdir -p "$WINEPREFIX"

# Check DLL Linkage
${TARGET_HOST}-objdump -p "${INSTALL}/openocd/bin/openocd.exe" | grep "DLL Name"

# Test openocd.exe version output
xvfb-run -a --server-args="-screen 0 1024x768x24" \
    wine "${INSTALL}/openocd/bin/openocd.exe" --version

```

### 8. Packaging

To create a standalone `.zip` archive containing `openocd.exe`, drivers, scripts, and bundled DLLs:

```bash
cd "${RISCV_ROOT}/install/openocd"
zip -r9 "${RISCV_ROOT}/install/riscv64-openocd-V_2.1-windows.zip" ./*

```